  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded with no alerts.\n\n**1. What the data holds**\n- Data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` covers `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`. There are 14,074 web requests, dated 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03.\n- Each request records the client IP, URL, response code, response size in bytes, source and destination country, OS, agent, referer and tags.\n- The response code is stored as text, not a number. Only three codes occur: 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441). So \"5xx\" here means 503.\n- Source country is \"US\" for every request, so it's useless for charts. I used destination country instead.\n\n**2. Dashboard `EFF-77649760 Web logs overview`** (id `9bbf3ffe-bce6-4ef8-ba6a-8bc6293ddcd8`, default time range last 7 days), 8 panels:\n- **Headline numbers** (these use the existing data view): total requests, unique visitors (by client IP), average response size, and 5xx error rate.\n- **Charts** (these use ES|QL queries):\n  - requests over time, stacked by response code\n  - top 10 requested URLs\n  - top 10 destination countries\n  - bytes transferred over time\n\nI checked the chart queries against the data and they return results. I haven't opened the dashboard in the browser, so I haven't seen the panels drawn.\n\n**3. Rule `EFF-77649760 5xx spike`** (id `6e2fc675-8026-4454-b240-d45af096d242`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- It has no notification actions yet, so alerts only appear in Kibana. Add an email or Slack action if you want to be notified.\n- 503s average about 7 a day in this data, so I don't expect it to fire under normal traffic.\n\nThe request bodies are saved as `dash.py` and `rule.json` in my scratchpad directory.",
